---
// THE FOUR WINDS SYNOPSIS DECK (rebuilt 2026-09-01 to Andrea Pomarico's
// request, relayed by the founder): each nonprofit in the stewardship
// circle provides a brief synopsis — what the org is and how it fits the
// Four Winds puzzle, a bio of the founder, who it will assist and how, and
// why Four Winds is the ideal location. Andrea's fundraising guidance:
// LEAD with equine-assisted therapy for first responders and veterans
// ("8,700 veterans in Castle Rock alone" — her figure), introduce the
// platform gently through AI SCHEDULING ("breathing time for the land,
// animals, and practitioners"), and SAVE the mycelium/Current-cy economy
// for later — "there will be time for introducing that awesome and
// mind-bending piece later once things get rolling."
//
// Deliberately NOT on the WeavePitch shell: the shared opening leads with
// exactly the novel-economy story Andrea asked to defer. Same visual
// vocabulary, her structure. Styles below mirror WeavePitch's w-classes.
//
// ⚠ NO FINANCIAL CONTENT (standing rule). The 8,700-veterans figure is
// Andrea's own, used at her direction.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Lichen at Four Winds"
  description="Lichen's synopsis for the Four Winds stewardship circle: equine-assisted care for first responders and veterans, and the scheduling layer that gives land, animals and practitioners room to breathe."
>
  <main>
    <section class="container container--narrow fw">
      <span class="fw-for">Lichen at Four Winds · for the stewardship circle</span>
      <h1 class="display h-1">Care that begins with a horse,<br />and a calendar that protects everyone giving it.</h1>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Lichen, in brief</h3>
      <p class="wbody">
        Lichen is a nonprofit healing network — a care model and the
        technology that runs it. Members are held by small care teams;
        practitioners, spaces and programs share one platform for
        scheduling, membership and coordination; and donors fund
        subsidized care through a published, transparent policy. In the
        Four Winds puzzle, Lichen is two pieces: a <strong>care
        partner</strong> bringing trauma-informed, equine-assisted
        programming, and the <strong>coordination layer</strong> that lets
        a many-organization sanctuary run as one calm, legible whole.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Who&rsquo;s behind it</h3>
      <p class="wbody">
        <strong>Galyn Burke</strong>, Lichen&rsquo;s founder, is a tech
        professional turned trauma therapist — a licensed Marriage and
        Family Therapist and counselor across Colorado, California and
        Washington, working with anxiety, depression and PTSD. Before
        Lichen, she ran an integrated clinical, equine and land-based
        healing center, and she founded Lichen after a decade in
        technology to build the infrastructure that kind of care has
        always deserved. At Four Winds she wears both hats: clinician
        shaping the care programming, and builder of the platform
        beneath it.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Who Lichen will assist, and how</h3>
      <p class="wbody">
        <strong>First responders and veterans, first.</strong> Some 8,700
        veterans live in Castle Rock alone — and for many of them, and for
        the firefighters, medics and officers alongside them, a clinic
        waiting room is exactly the wrong door. Equine-assisted therapy is
        the right one: a horse asks for regulation before conversation,
        honesty before analysis, and presence before anything at all.
        Lichen&rsquo;s care model wraps that session in a team — a
        therapist, the horse, and the coordination that makes the next
        step obvious — so a first visit becomes a path, not a one-off.
      </p>
      <p class="wbody">
        The same doors open to trauma survivors, caregivers running on
        empty, young people who need a reason to show up somewhere, and
        families healing together — the people Four Winds has already
        served for fifteen years. Lichen&rsquo;s published subsidy policy
        means ability to pay never decides who gets to stand in the
        arena.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">The scheduling layer — efficiency, and breathing room</h3>
      <p class="wbody">
        The gentle first taste of Lichen&rsquo;s platform is a calendar
        that finally matches how a sanctuary actually works. Every room,
        arena, pasture, animal and practitioner has a real schedule;
        sessions are booked through stewards who approve each request; and
        AI helps find the times that work across many organizations
        without a single spreadsheet or double-booked barn.
      </p>
      <p class="wbody">
        Just as important is what the calendar refuses to give away:
        <strong>breathing time</strong>. On Lichen, nobody is bookable by
        default — a horse&rsquo;s day has a ceiling, a pasture&rsquo;s
        recovery is scheduled as firmly as a session, and a
        practitioner&rsquo;s rest is protected structurally, not left to
        their willpower. Maximum efficiency and mandatory rest, in the
        same system — for the land, the animals, and the people who care
        for both.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Why Four Winds</h3>
      <p class="wbody">
        Thirty-five acres against the Front Range, between Pikes Peak and
        Longs Peak. The 1899 Wolfensberger barn, whose acoustics are said
        to rival Carnegie Hall. Pastures, ponds, ancient pines and dark
        skies. Horses, llamas, goats and a wild mustang, alongside the
        deer, elk, fox and owls that were already here. A regenerative
        farm with agricultural status, an organic garden and greenhouse,
        lodging and practitioner housing, and fifteen years of programming
        for veterans, first responders, trauma survivors and people with
        special needs — plus KAHUBA&rsquo;s seventeen years of sanctuary
        work on the same ground. Equine-assisted care needs healed land,
        seasoned animals, and a community that already knows how to hold
        people. Four Winds is not a promising site for this work;
        it is the rare place where the work is already true.
      </p>

      <p class="wcallout">
        Horses and humans, healing each other on land that can&rsquo;t be
        sold out from under the work.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">And beneath it all, quietly</h3>
      <p class="wbody">
        There is a deeper layer to Lichen — an economy that counts every
        contribution, human and otherwise, so the generosity that keeps a
        place like Four Winds alive is finally visible. It will matter
        enormously, later, once things get rolling. For now it simply
        hums underneath, and the curious can read more at
        <a href="/about" class="wlink">lichen.health/about</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .fw { padding-block: var(--s-9); }
  .fw-for {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-sm);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--peach-deep);
  }
  .fw .h-1 { margin-top: var(--s-3); }
  /* Mirror of WeavePitch's shared content classes, so this page reads as a
     sibling of every other deck without carrying the shell's opening. */
  .wsub { margin-top: var(--s-9); }
  .wbody {
    font-size: var(--t-lg); line-height: var(--lh-normal);
    color: var(--ink-soft); font-weight: 300; margin-top: var(--s-4);
  }
  .wbody strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 500; }
  .wbody + .wbody { margin-top: var(--s-4); }
  .wlink { color: var(--rust); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .wcallout {
    margin-top: var(--s-7); margin-bottom: 0; padding: var(--s-5) var(--s-6);
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-style: italic;
    font-size: 22px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--peach); background: var(--peach-tint);
    border-radius: 0 var(--r-md) var(--r-md) 0;
  }
</style>
